\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Chirality and the scope of other doubling remedies}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{In even dimension #{d=2m}, the specified Hermitian gamma
matrices define a chirality operator on #{S\otimes F} by
##{C=i^m\Gamma_1\cdots\Gamma_{2m}.}
Write #{P=\Gamma_1\cdots\Gamma_{2m}}. Reversing the order
uses #{m(2m-1)} interchanges, hence
#{P^*=(-1)^mP} and #{P^2=(-1)^mI}. It follows that
#{C^*=C} and #{C^2=I}. Moving one gamma matrix across the
other #{2m-1} factors gives #{C\Gamma_j=-\Gamma_j C}.
Since the links act only on #{F}, #{C} commutes with each shift
and with #{L_{a,U}}. Therefore
##{\begin{aligned}
CD_{a,U}+D_{a,U}C&=0,\\
CD_{W,a,U}+D_{W,a,U}C&=-ra\,C L_{a,U}.
\end{aligned}}
The second expression is generally nonzero: already at trivial
links the nearest-neighbor Laplacian is nonzero on a nonconstant
Fourier mode. Thus the Wilson term changes this exact finite
anticommutation relation, the algebraic chiral cost discussed in
\citet{Section 9.3, p. 21}{wingate2025lattice}.}

\p{Other approaches change different data. The following are
source comparisons, not additional equivalence theorems for
these finite operators.}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Staggered fields.} Spin diagonalization uses a
site-dependent product of gamma matrices and then a one-component
field with alternating difference signs
\citet{Section 9.4, Equations (9.19)–(9.25), pp. 22–23}{wingate2025lattice}.
The conventional four-dimensional count is 4 tastes relative
to minimal four-component Dirac spinors. It is not the number
of exact zero modes on a finite grid. In dimension #{4} the
present #{S} has 16 complex components; no taste identification
for that reducible carrier is asserted.
On a periodic torus the site-parity transformation also requires
compatible even periods or correspondingly changed boundary
conditions. No global spin diagonalization on arbitrary odd
periods, rooting assertion or continuum equivalence is proved here.}
\li{\strong{Dirac–Kähler fields.} A geometric route places
components on cells of different degrees. Becher and Joos map
forms to cochains by integration over cells and use the boundary
and coboundary correspondences to write a lattice equation
\citet{Section 3.1, Equations (3.1)–(3.4), pp. 352–353}{becher1982dirac}.
This is taken up in [Dirac–Kähler operators](fdip-0091).
A dimension count does not identify that construction with the
present spinor/internal operator, and no such identification or
optional cochain construction is made here.}
\li{\strong{Ginsparg–Wilson operators.} The modified relation
##{C\mathcal D+\mathcal D C=a\mathcal D C\mathcal D}
replaces exact anticommutation
\citet{Section 9.6, Equation (9.27), p. 24}{wingate2025lattice}.
If #{\mathcal D} is invertible, multiplication on both sides
by its inverse gives
##{C\mathcal D^{-1}+\mathcal D^{-1}C=aC,}
matching the source's contact-term identity (9.28).
Neither relation constructs an overlap operator here or follows
from the Wilson kernel theorem.}
}

\p{These finite statements do not derive a positive fermion
transfer operator or an interacting continuum theory. The
[reflection-positive transfer construction](fdip-007L) has its
own hypotheses; the choice #{r=1} does not extend it to a coupled
Wilson-fermion action.}
